    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 4 USER]\ne031\tThere is history here.\ne032\tEarly on we said major structural decisions would be mutual.\ne033\tIn practice, Jonah made many of them because he was in the session every day, and I usually trusted him.\ne034\tNow he says I am reopening the structure at the last moment after being less available.\ne035\tThat is partly true.\ne036\tI was reporting another project for two months.\ne037\tBut I also think “you were not in the room” can turn into permanent final say.\n[Turn 4 ASSISTANT]\ne038\tThe informal agreement and the working reality diverged.\ne039\tJonah accumulated practical authorship through sustained editing, while you did not explicitly surrender mutual authority.\ne040\tBoth facts deserve recognition.\ne041\tA fair process now may give greater weight to his knowledge of the cut without treating that as unilateral ownership.\ne042\tThe harder question is what mutual decision means under a deadline: consensus on every change, a defined area of final say, or a rule for what happens when you remain split.\n[Turn 5 USER]\ne043\tA curator from the showcase listened yesterday.\ne044\tShe loved the ending and said it was the first time the piece felt inevitable.\ne045\tThat word bothered me, but it also means Jonah achieved something.\ne046\tShe can move us to a later program, though she cannot guarantee the same room or promotion.\ne047\tJonah thinks postponing would throw away momentum for a concern the participant may not share.\ne048\tI am starting to wonder whether I am asking the piece to preserve every ambiguity because I am afraid of making an argument.\n[Turn 5 ASSISTANT]\ne049\tDocumentaries do make arguments through selection, and preserving every hesitation can be another form of distortion.\ne050\tThe curator's response is useful evidence about the ending's force, not evidence that its interpretation is fair.\ne051\tThe later program reduces the deadline's finality but introduces real opportunity costs.\ne052\tYou might distinguish changes required for representational integrity from changes that would make the argument feel more like yours.\ne053\tThat distinction will not be perfectly objective, but trying to articulate it may clarify the disagreement.",
